rency that are not formally declared. Each correct change preserves existing invariants but introduces new ones, so the invariant count grows linearly with changes while the ability to reason about all of them simultaneously decays combinatorially.", "probability": 0.08}, {"conjecture": "Change footprints grow because each locally-correct modification has a nonzero probability of converting a previously stateless component into a stateful one, and stateful components require coordinated changes across all callers; the number of such components grows over time, increasing the fraction of changes that require multi-site coordination.", "probability": 0.04}, {"conjecture": "The cost of a change is proportional to the number of semantic boundaries it crosses, and each past change tends to sharpen rather than smooth these boundaries; after enough changes the system develops rigid interfaces that are individually clean but collectively form a maze that every new change must navigate.", "probability": 0.05}, {"conjecture": "Each reviewed and tested change encodes a local decision that becomes a precedent for future changes, so the system accumulates decision-specific compatibility constraints at a rate proportional to the change rate, and each new change must respect a growing set of precedents—making the effective change footprint grow as O(N) in the number of prior changes.", "probability": 0.07}, {"conjecture": "Tested changes create a ratchet effect: each test suite that passes becomes a constraint that future changes must not violate, so the number of test-inforced constraints grows linearly with changes while the probability that a new change intersects any given constraint grows with the constraint count, making the expected number of constraints touched per change scale as O(N^2).", "probability": 0.06}]}
